## The Idea

I wanted a retro console on my phone that felt like holding the real thing, not like opening one more app full of ads.

- **Make It Physical:** Bring back the small rituals of a handheld, like choosing a cartridge and watching it power on.
- **Keep It Honest:** No ads, no account and no tracking.
- **Make It Work Anywhere:** Everything lives inside the app, so games play offline, with no internet.

## Designing the Handheld Feeling

### From Mockups to Screens

I started with my own mockups for the Home, Games and Play screens, with a lavender handheld look that carries through the whole app. Games live on a shelf as cartridges, organized by console.

### Small Rituals

The experience depends on details. Inserting a cartridge plays a short sequence, a startup screen follows, and the on-screen buttons vibrate when you press them. There are in-game saves like an original cartridge, plus Save and Load at any moment.

![Three RetroPoket Color screens: the play screen with physical-feeling buttons, Bluetooth controller remapping, and the skin store](/images/projects/retropoket-play-settings-store.webp)

## Building It With Claude Code

This is where my workflow moved past the Figma file. I built the app with Claude Code, working from my own designs and testing on a real phone along the way.

- **Three Consoles in One:** The app plays Pocket (.gb, .gbc), Pocket Advance (.gba) and 16-bit Console (.sfc, .smc) games, with the emulators bundled inside.
- **Bluetooth Controllers:** Players can pair a controller and remap any button. I tested it with a Luna controller.
- **A Fair Business Model:** The app is free, and optional skins are a one-time purchase.
- **No Games Included:** People play copies of cartridges they own, or free homebrew games from indie developers.

## Where It Is Now

- **Android:** In closed testing on Google Play, with friends testing it before release.
- **iPhone:** A free web version that runs in Safari and can be added to the home screen.
- **Website:** A product page at anitools.dev that explains the app and invites new testers.
